WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.765 --> 00:00:05.616
<v><b>Speaker 1:</b></v> <b>British and American English</b> &#x2013; the <i>same</i>, but <i>different</i>

2
00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:14.074
<b>British and American people speak the same language &#x2013; English &#x2013; but with some small differences.</b>

3
00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:17.135
1 Vocabulary

4
00:00:17.911 --> 00:00:25.466
Some words are different in American English, for example, they say <i>zip code</i> not <i>postcode</i>,

5
00:00:25.522 --> 00:00:30.414
<i>vacation</i> not <i>holiday</i>, and <i>elevator</i> not <i>lift</i>.

6
00:00:31.249 --> 00:00:36.455
Some words have different meanings, for example, in British English,

7
00:00:36.496 --> 00:00:45.146
a <i>purse</i> is a wallet for women, for money and bank cards. In American English, a <i>purse</i> is a bag.

8
00:00:46.539 --> 00:00:49.081
<v><b>Speaker 2:</b></v> 2 Spelling

9
00:00:49.354 --> 00:01:00.969
<i>Colour</i>, <i>favour</i>, and other words that end in <i>-our</i> in British English end in <i>-or</i> in American English.

10
00:01:01.297 --> 00:01:14.797
<i>Centre</i>, <i>theatre</i>, and other words that end in <i>-tre</i> in British English end in <i>-ter</i> in American English.

11
00:01:16.218 --> 00:01:18.923
<v><b>Speaker 1:</b></v> 3 Grammar

12
00:01:19.415 --> 00:01:23.924
There are some small differences, especially prepositions.

13
00:01:24.074 --> 00:01:32.040
For example, Americans say <i>See you Friday</i>, but British people say <i>See you on Friday</i>.

14
00:01:33.407 --> 00:01:37.014
<v><b>Speaker 2:</b></v> 4 Pronunciation

15
00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:44.147
This is the really important difference between American and British English.

16
00:01:44.584 --> 00:01:50.077
American accents and British accents are very different.

17
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:59.177
When Americans start speaking, British people know they are American, and vice versa.